Now I understand the key issue. Let me look at the `chunk_array` definition more carefully. It uses `DEFINE_ARRAY_TYPE` (not `DEFINE_ARRAY_TYPE_INLINEFIRST`), so it uses heap-only allocation:

```c
DEFINE_ARRAY_TYPE(chunk_array, struct chunk)
```

This means `chunk_array_append` calls `lwan_array_append_heap`, which reallocs every 16 elements.

Now let me look at the `post_process_template` function again. The critical issue is in the `ACTION_START_ITER` handling:

```c
for (prev_chunk = chunk;; chunk++) {
    if (chunk->action == ACTION_LAST) {
        lwan_status_error("Internal error: Could not find the end iter chunk");
        return false;
    }
    if (chunk->action == ACTION_END_ITER) {
        size_t start_index = (size_t)chunk->data;
        size_t prev_index =
            chunk_array_get_elem_index(&parser->chunks, prev_chunk);

        if (prev_index == start_index) {
            chunk->flags |= flags;
            chunk->data =
                chunk_array_get_elem(&parser->chunks, start_index);
            break;
        }
    }
}
```

The loop increments `chunk` past the array bounds if there's no matching `ACTION_END_ITER` with the right `start_index`. But wait, the `ACTION_LAST` sentinel should always be there...

